{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean head ff04227f442cac6eb3fd3e64507e5da8cb234918 on task/kernel-key-rotation-integration.\n\nThis head answers the single finding of my earlier changes-requested verdict on 264a1323, and it answers it by carrying rotations through the checkpoint rather than by refusing to write one or by documenting the limit. That was the largest of the three options I offered and it is the right one.\n\nGates at this head: go vet ./... clean; go test ./... green across all thirteen packages, kernel at 129s; go test -race ./... green; go build ./... ok; checkout clean at the exact head; current main 0a66e859 is an ancestor.\n\nCIRCULAR TRUST, which the request asked me to look at hardest. It is avoided, and the structure is right. verifyCheckpointRotations begins at desc.SequencerPublicKey - the genesis descriptor, read from the genesis commit, not from the checkpoint - then walks the rotation commits of the real ref sequence returned by RevListMetadata, authenticating each transition under the key current immediately before it, refusing a no-op rotation and a non-empty rotation tree, and only then returning the frontier key. loadCheckpoint uses that derived key to authenticate the checkpoint commit. Nothing the checkpoint asserts contributes to deriving the key that authenticates it. The rotations are read from actual commit messages in the sequence, not from cached payload.\n\nBOUNDS. stored.Depth is attacker-controlled payload and indexes into sequence, but validateCheckpoint runs first and refuses len(sequence) \u003c stored.Depth+1 along with the single-parent chaining checks, so the rotation walk cannot run off the end. I checked the call order in loadCheckpoint rather than assuming it.\n\nMY FINDING IS CLOSED, verified rather than read. Probe at this exact head in a scratch worktree: submit, rotate, submit, publish a checkpoint whose cached prefix spans the rotation, then cold restart. Result checkpoint=true depth=3 events=2 - consumed, with depth counting the rotation and events not. At 264a1323 the same probe reported checkpoint=false.\n\nA CORRECTION TO MY OWN REASONING, recorded because it changes what this review can claim. I expected this change to also close a forged-checkpoint hole at the previous head, on the theory that a holder of a retired key could have signed a checkpoint that was authenticated against the genesis descriptor key. I ran that probe against 264a1323 before asserting it, and it was refused there too - but for an unrelated reason: the depth-versus-events mismatch rejected the checkpoint before any key question was reached. The bug was providing the protection accidentally.\n\nThat makes the correct statement narrower and more interesting than the one I nearly signed. This fix removes the accidental protection, because a rotation-spanning checkpoint is now accepted on its own terms, and it has to replace it with a deliberate one. It does: probe at this head shows a checkpoint published with the retired key is refused on cold restart, checkpoint=false, because the derived frontier key no longer matches the signature. The property is preserved deliberately where it was previously preserved by accident. That is the substantive security content of this change and it holds.\n\nONE OBSERVATION, not a finding. Authentication of the checkpoint commit now necessarily happens after its payload is decoded, because deriving the frontier key needs stored.Depth, which lives in the payload. The previous code verified the signature before reading anything. This is inherent to the design rather than an oversight - you cannot know which prefix to walk without reading the claim first - and it is bounded by the 256 MB ReadFileLimit, cross-checked against the real commit sequence, and reachable only by an actor who can already write refs/gitseq/checkpoints in the repository. I am not blocking on it, but the ordering did change and a future reader deserves to know that the decoder now runs on unauthenticated input.\n\nThe conditions I checked and found met: rotation-chain trust as above; frontier-key checkpoint authentication as above; retired-writer and forged-signature refusal, probed; post-rotation cold restart and resident delta, probed and covered by the head's own tests; event-versus-depth honesty, depth counts rotations and events does not, and the checkpoint's Events field now derives from len(stored.Events) rather than Depth; fallback semantics unchanged, every checkpoint failure still returns ErrNoUsableCheckpoint and falls back to the full audit; docs union in docs/reference.md and spike/README.md; Rests-On trailers present on every commit; and the full gates above.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:479325db85492ee1ef703b3fdb8043e9d2ead000","head":"ff04227f442cac6eb3fd3e64507e5da8cb234918","verdict":"approved"}}